      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="skip-level">How to run a skip-level conversation that actually moves something</h2>
  <p class="line">
    A skip-level meeting — a direct, structured conversation with your manager's manager — is one of the most underused tools available inside most companies, precisely because it feels like a bold move. It is not a complaint session and not an exit interview. Used well, it is a chance to get a second, more senior read on whether the blocker is your manager or the structure, and it puts your name in front of someone who has more influence over headcount decisions than your direct manager does.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Step 1 — Set up the meeting through the normal channel.</strong> Ask your manager first, framed as wanting broader visibility into the team's direction, not as going around them. Most managers who are not the actual problem will support this without friction.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Step 2 — Bring one clear ask, not a list of grievances.</strong> A useful frame: "Based on what you've seen across the org, what would it take for someone in my role to move to the next level here, and is that realistically available in this team?" That question does double duty — it signals ambition and it surfaces the structural answer directly.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Step 3 — Let them talk more than you do.</strong> The value of a skip-level is what a more senior person tells you about the bigger picture — team budget, upcoming restructuring, plans you cannot see from your seat. Listen for those signals; they answer the Layer 3 question better than any internal job board.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Step 4 — Follow up in writing within a few days.</strong> A short note referencing what was discussed and any commitment made keeps it real. If a commitment was made — a stretch project, a review at the next cycle — this note is what you point back to later.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Step 5 — Give it one full review cycle, then reassess.</strong> If nothing has moved by the next formal calibration, you have your answer on Layer 2 and Layer 3 together — and it is time to weigh an internal lateral move or an external search seriously.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="internal-mobility">Internal mobility: the lateral move most people never ask for</h2>
  <p class="line">
    If Layer 3 checks out — real roles do exist somewhere in the company — a lateral move is worth taking seriously before you look outside, and the research on this is more encouraging than most people expect. Studies on internal mobility have found that lateral moves are one of the strongest predictors of employees staying with a company, and that people who move laterally are subsequently promoted at a noticeably higher rate than those who stay put, because the new scope and visibility compound over time. Despite that, most employees never ask: research on internal opportunity consistently finds a large gap between how many people say they would consider a new internal role and how many actually pursue a lateral one, largely because a sideways move still feels, wrongly, like a step back.
  </p>
  <p class="line">
    Career growth is now a top-tier retention factor for employees in India specifically, which is why more mid-size and large employers have formalised internal job boards, mentorship-linked mobility programs, and cross-functional project rotations over the last few years — not as a favour, but because replacing and onboarding external hires costs more than moving someone who already knows the systems and culture. If your company has any version of this, it is worth checking before assuming you have to leave to grow.
  </p>
  <div class="card">
    <p class="line"><strong>Honest take:</strong> A lateral move only fixes a content or structural plateau if it comes with a genuinely different skill or scope attached — moving sideways into a role that is functionally the same job with a different team name does not break a plateau, it just relocates it. Before you apply, name the specific new capability the move would force you to build. If you cannot name one, keep looking.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="when-to-conclude">When to conclude the company itself has a real ceiling</h2>
  <p class="line">
    This is the hardest call, and it should come last — after you have honestly worked through Layers 1 and 2, not before. A structural plateau is real, not a personal failure, when several of these hold true at the same time: a hiring freeze has been in place for more than one full quarter with no communicated end date, internal job postings above your level have been essentially empty for two quarters running, your function or business unit has been flat or shrinking rather than growing, and a skip-level conversation produced sympathy but no concrete commitment or timeline.
  </p>
  <p class="line">
    None of that means you did something wrong. A flat organisation with genuinely no room above you cannot be solved by working harder inside it — that is the definition of a structural plateau in the research, and it needs an external or cross-functional fix, not more personal effort. If your honest audit lands here, the responsible next step is to keep performing well while you look elsewhere, not to quietly disengage and hope the situation resolves itself.
  </p>

  <h2 id="external-offer-leverage">Using an external offer as leverage, without actually needing to leave</h2>
  <p class="line">
    A genuine external offer is one of the fastest ways to get a stalled internal conversation moving — but only when it is used honestly. Interviewing while employed is not disloyal; it is how you find out your real market value, and that number is often the single most persuasive piece of evidence in a plateau conversation.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Get the market read even if you are not sure you want to leave.</strong> Two or three real interviews tell you whether your current pay and scope have actually fallen behind the market, or whether the plateau is only about growth, not money.</li>
    <li><strong>Bring the offer to your manager as information, not an ultimatum.</strong> "I've had this offer and I'd rather stay if there's a real path to X" reads very differently from a threat, and most managers respond better to the former.</li>
    <li><strong>Be genuinely willing to take the offer.</strong> A counter-offer accepted without real internal change tends to repeat the same plateau within a year — the underlying content, manager, or structural layer usually has not actually moved, only the number on the payslip has.</li>
    <li><strong>Watch for a counter-offer that fixes pay but not scope.</strong> If the raise arrives but the work, ceiling, and advocacy stay exactly the same, the plateau is still there — it is just quieter for a while.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="mistakes">Mistakes that stretch a plateau out longer than it needs to be</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Assuming it's the company before checking Layer 1 and 2.</strong> Jumping straight to "this place has no growth" without a skill audit or a direct manager conversation means you may quit a company that would have moved you, over a fixable gap.</li>
    <li><strong>Assuming it's you when it's actually the company.</strong> Blaming yourself for a genuinely flat org chart leads to burning out trying to "earn" a promotion slot that does not structurally exist yet.</li>
    <li><strong>Quiet quitting instead of diagnosing.</strong> Doing the bare minimum feels like protection, but it removes the very evidence — new skill, new scope, visible effort — you would need to make any of the three fixes actually work.</li>
    <li><strong>Treating a skip-level as a one-time fix.</strong> One conversation rarely changes headcount reality by itself. It is a data-gathering step and a visibility step, not a guaranteed outcome.</li>
    <li><strong>Waiting indefinitely for "next year."</strong> If the same promise has repeated across two review cycles with no concrete milestone attached, that pattern is itself the answer to the structural-layer question.</li>
  </ul>

